IS  - 1810-6838 (Linking)
VI  - 22
IP  - 1
DP  - 2026 Jan
TI  - What is new in pleural infections?
PG  - 250038
LID - 10.1183/20734735.0038-2025 [doi]
LID - 250038
AB  - The field of pleural medicine has seen significant advances in recent years, 
      shaping both the current and future evaluation and treatment of pleural 
      infection. Recent studies have demonstrated a continued rise in the prevalence of 
      pleural infection and provide a more refined understanding of the risk factors 
      associated with its occurrence. The increased use of point-of-care ultrasound and 
      the identification of computed tomography markers have enhanced and expedited 
      initial evaluation. Novel microbiological techniques are expanding our 
      understanding of the diversity of causative pathogens and disease mechanisms, 
      with potential implications for diagnostic strategies and antibiotic selection. 
      Emerging studies are also exploring the feasibility of shorter courses of 
      antibiotics. Meanwhile, novel blood and pleural fluid biomarkers are being 
      increasingly studied for both diagnosis and prognostication. Furthermore, 
      accumulating evidence on risk stratification is increasingly being published and 
      used in clinical settings. The specific role, optimal sequence and patient 
      selection for intrapleural enzyme therapy, surgery, and medical thoracoscopy is 
      enabling a more personalised approach to treatment and may improve patient 
      outcomes. These and other developments are discussed in this review, which 
      presents a state-of-the-art overview of pleural infection management from the 
      clinician's perspective, highlighting recent and anticipated advances.
