TI  - Role of Single Port Rigid Thoracoscopy in Undiagnosed Pleural Effusion.
PG  - 194-199
LID - 10.4046/trd.2023.0102 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: In recent years, medical thoracoscopy has been well established to 
      play an important role in undiagnosed pleural effusion; however, this procedure 
      is underutilized due to limited availability of the instruments it requires. This 
      study analysed the outcome of single port rigid thoracoscopy in patients with 
      undiagnosed pleural effusions. METHODS: This study retrospectively analysed the 
      outcomes of all patients with undiagnosed pleural effusion presenting to our 
      centre between 2016 to 2020 who underwent single port rigid medical thoracoscopy 
      as a diagnostic procedure. RESULTS: In total, 92 patients underwent single port 
      rigid medical thoracoscopy. The most common presenting symptom was shortness of 
      breath. A majority of the patients had lymphocytic exudative pleural effusion. 
      The average biopsy sample size was 18 mm, and no major complication was reported 
      in any of the patients. CONCLUSION: Single port rigid thoracoscopy is a safe and 
      well-tolerated procedure that yields a biopsy of a larger size with high 
      diagnostic yield. Moreover, the low cost of the instruments required by this 
      procedure makes it particularly suited for use in developing countries.
