DP  - 2017 Sep
TI  - Propionibacterium acnes pleural empyema following medical thoracoscopy.
PG  - e00249
LID - 10.1002/rcr2.249 [doi]
LID - e00249
AB  - Propionibacterium acnes (P. acnes) is a Gram-positive anaerobic rod and a common 
      skin commensal that colonizes sebaceous glands. It has infrequently been 
      associated with invasive opportunistic infections and can cause 
      implant-associated infections through a biofilm mode of growth. Medical 
      thoracoscopy is a common procedure for diagnosis and treatment of exudative 
      pleural effusions; empyema is a recognized complication. We present a rare case 
      of P. acnes pleural empyema 3 weeks following medical thoracoscopy and subsequent 
      intercostal drain insertion for 3 days in a 75-year-old man. We postulate that 
      pathogenesis may be associated with inoculation at the time of thoracoscopy or 
      via biofilm formation on the intercostal drain. This case highlights the 
      potential for this pathogen to cause clinically significant disease following 
      thoracoscopy and pleural drainage.
