DP  - 2003 May-Jun
TI  - Ultrasound guidance for medical thoracoscopy: a novel approach.
PG  - 299-301
AB  - BACKGROUND: Commonly, a pneumothorax is induced before medical thoracoscopy to 
      facilitate safe entry into the pleural space. OBJECTIVE: Evaluate the use of 
      transthoracic ultrasound to locate a safe entry site for trocar placement during 
      medical thoracoscopy without induction of a preprocedure pneumothorax. METHOD: 
      The study was designed as a prospective cohort study, performed in the setting of 
      a tertiary care hospital with an active interventional pulmonology program. It 
      included 20 consecutive patients referred for medical thoracoscopy. RESULTS: 
      Ultrasound identified entry sites in all 20 patients. All sites were successfully 
      used, despite the presence of adhesions in 3 patients. There were no 
      complications. CONCLUSIONS: Ultrasound could safely and reliably identify entry 
      sites for trocar placement during medical thoracoscopy, even in patients with 
      pleural adhesions. The use of ultrasound may replace the practice of pneumothorax 
      induction before medical thoracoscopy.
