TI  - Pleural effusion guidelines from ICS and NCCP Section 1: Basic principles, 
      laboratory tests and pleural procedures.
PG  - 230-248
LID - 10.4103/lungindia.lungindia_33_24 [doi]
AB  - Pleural effusion is a common problem in our country, and most of these patients 
      need invasive tests as they can't be evaluated by blood tests alone. The simplest 
      of them is diagnostic pleural aspiration, and diagnostic techniques such as 
      medical thoracoscopy are being performed more frequently than ever before. 
      However, most physicians in India treat pleural effusion empirically, leading to 
      delays in diagnosis, misdiagnosis and complications from wrong treatments. This 
      situation must change, and the adoption of evidence-based protocols is urgently 
      needed. Furthermore, the spectrum of pleural disease in India is different from 
      that in the West, and yet Western guidelines and algorithms are used by Indian 
      physicians. Therefore, India-specific consensus guidelines are needed. To fulfil 
      this need, the Indian Chest Society and the National College of Chest Physicians; 
      the premier societies for pulmonary physicians came together to create this 
      National guideline. This document aims to provide evidence based recommendations 
      on basic principles, initial assessment, diagnostic modalities and management of 
      pleural effusions.
