TI  - The application of probe-based confocal laser endomicroscopy in pleural diseases.
PG  - e48996
LID - 10.1097/MD.0000000000048996 [doi]
LID - e48996
AB  - Probe-based confocal laser endomicroscopy (pCLE) constitutes a cutting-edge in 
      vivo imaging technology, yet its application within the realm of respiratory 
      medicine, especially of pleural disease, is still in its incipient phase. This 
      single-center, retrospective analysis encompassed patients admitted to our 
      department between August 2023 and January 2024 for the investigation of "pleural 
      effusion of unknown etiology," who subsequently underwent medical thoracoscopy 
      augmented by pCLE examination. The study culminated in a comprehensive analysis 
      of the pCLE imaging attributes of pleural lesions, integrating cytological and 
      histopathological findings to delineate the characteristics of benign vs 
      malignant pleural diseases. A total of 18 patients were included in the study (5 
      cases of lung adenocarcinoma, 10 cases of tuberculous pleuritis, and 3 cases of 
      parapneumonic effusion), with 44 valid pCLE video recordings collected for final 
      data analysis. The analysis of qualitative criteria for pCLE imaging revealed 
      that the size, morphology, and fluorescence consistency of cells were effective 
      in distinguishing between benign and malignant pleural diseases (P < .001). The 
      quantitative criteria analysis indicated that the mean cell size in malignant 
      pleural diseases was significantly larger than that in benign conditions, while 
      the cell density was lower in malignant cases compared to benign ones. No 
      significant difference was observed in the maximum vessel diameter between the 2. 
      Additionally, differentiating pCLE images between tuberculous pleural effusion 
      and parapneumonic effusion proved to be challenging. The findings indicate that 
      pCLE is viable modality for the examination of the pleural space, with distinct 
      imaging characteristics observed in benign vs malignant pleural diseases.
