TI  - Massive pulmonary embolism led to cardiac arrest two days after thoracoscopy in a 
      young male with pleural tuberculosis.
PG  - e01069
LID - 10.1002/rcr2.1069 [doi]
LID - e01069
AB  - TB itself is considered an independent risk factor for VTE; however, developing 
      pulmonary embolism after medical thoracoscopy is extremely rare. Herein, we 
      describe a 30-year-old previously healthy male with pleural tuberculosis 
      developed a massive pulmonary embolism with subsequent cardiac arrest after a 
      diagnostic medical thoracoscopy. Computed tomography pulmonary angiogram (CTPA) 
      showed major right pulmonary embolism (PE). Unfortunately, the patient passed 
      away despite resuscitation and extensive organ support in the intensive care unit 
      (ICU). This case highlights the thrombotic risk in this population group in order 
      to avoid such devastating complications.
